---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/advanced/contracts">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Contract law governs <strong>promises the law will enforce</strong> — and its genius is
    refusing to enforce most of them. Society is dense with promises (dinner plans, favours,
    hopes); courts enforce only bargains — exchanges where each side gives value on serious
    terms. The elements (offer, acceptance, consideration, intention, capacity, legality) are a
    filter that keeps the state's enforcement machinery out of casual life and available to
    commerce. Master the filter and its exceptions, and you can predict almost any commercial
    dispute's frame.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the formation elements, and what happens when someone breaks the deal" widget="equationStep" config={contractCfg} />

  <h2>Formation: agreement, surgically defined</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>offer</strong> is a definite promise to be bound on specified terms; an
    <strong>acceptance</strong> is unqualified assent (the mirror-image rule). The boundary
    concepts do the work. An <strong>invitation to treat</strong> — shop displays, advertisements,
    auction lots, most website listings — invites offers rather than making one
    (<em>Pharmaceutical Society v Boots</em>: the self-service till, not the shelf, is where the
    contract forms). A <strong>counter-offer kills the original offer</strong> (<em>Hyde v
    Wrench</em>: refuse 1,000, offer 950, then try to accept 1,000 — nothing to accept) and a
    mere request for information does not; these distinctions decide real disputes weekly. The
    <strong>postal rule</strong> (acceptance binds on posting — <em>Adams v Lindsell</em>, 1818)
    survives as the grandfather exception to the general rule that acceptance must be
    communicated; electronic contracting has effectively collapsed the question (acceptance is
    received when the server receives it — and consumers now have statutory regimes for online
    formation, cooling-off and withdrawal). <strong>The founding case is worth its fame:</strong>
    <em>Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball</em> (1893) — a company advertised £100 to anyone who caught
    influenza after using its device as directed, depositing £1,000 with a bank as proof of
    sincerity. Carlill caught flu. Held: the advertisement was a unilateral offer to the world,
    performance of the conditions was acceptance, the bank deposit showed intention to be bound,
    and using the product (at the company's invitation) was good consideration. Every element of
    formation lives in one case.
  </p>

  <h2>Consideration: the bargain test</h2>
  <p>
    Consideration is the doctrine that separates <strong>contracts from gifts</strong>: each
    party must give (or promise) something of value <em>in the eyes of the law</em>. Its
    refinements are where students stumble. <strong>Sufficiency, not adequacy:</strong> courts do
    not police price — a peppercorn can support a contract if genuinely agreed
    (<em>Chappell v Nestlé</em>: chocolate wrappers counted, because the promotion asked for
    them); the lesson is that value is what the parties treat as value. <strong>Past
    consideration is no consideration</strong> — you cannot enforce a reward promised after the
    act was done. <strong>Existing duty is no consideration</strong> (<em>Stilk v Myrick</em>:
    sailors already contracted to sail could not demand extra wages for doing it), with the
    major modern escape in <em>Williams v Roffey</em>: a promise of extra payment can be
    supported where performing an existing duty confers a <strong>practical benefit</strong>
    (here: avoiding penalty clauses for late completion). <strong>Part payment of a debt</strong>
    does not discharge it (<em>Foakes v Beer</em> — £1,000 of a £2,090 debt leaves the balance
    owing), unless the creditor's promise is supported by something new (a horse, a cheque on
    different terms — practical benefit again), or <strong>promissory estoppel</strong> steps
    in: a clear promise intended to affect legal relations, relied upon, can be enforced as a
    shield (not a sword) even without consideration — <em>Central London Property Trust v High
    Trees House</em> (1947), Denning's war-time rent reduction, the doctrine's founding moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "no signature, no contract." Writing is required only for
    specified transactions (land transfers, some guarantees, consumer credit); the common law's
    default is that <strong>any serious bargain can be oral</strong> — what changes without
    writing is evidence, not validity. The companion error: "consideration must be fair." It
    must exist; fairness is the vitiating-factors doctrines' business (below), not
    consideration's.
  </p>

  <h2>Terms: what was actually promised</h2>
  <p>
    Once formed, a contract's content divides by importance. <strong>Conditions</strong> (vital
    terms) entitle the innocent party to terminate and claim damages on breach;
    <strong>warranties</strong> (secondary terms) entitle damages only;
    <strong>innominate terms</strong> (<em>Hong Kong Fir</em>: seaworthiness breached so late and
    trivially that the charterer's termination was itself repudiatory) get classified by the
    <em>effect</em> of the breach — the modern solution to the binary's rigidity.
    <strong>Implied terms</strong> fill gaps: by statute (the Sale of Goods Act/Consumer Rights
    Act 2015 implies satisfactory quality, fitness for purpose, and conformity with description —
    rights consumers cannot contract out of), by custom, and by the court's officious-bystander
    or business-efficacy tests (terms so obvious the parties would have said yes). <strong>Exclusion
    clauses</strong> face a gauntlet: incorporation (was the clause actually in the contract —
    the ticket cases), construction (does it cover <em>this</em> loss — the contra proferentem
    reading against the drafter), and statutory control (UCTA reasonableness; UCTA and the CRA
    2015 make some exclusions void outright — a business cannot exclude liability for death or
    personal injury from negligence, period). Consumer law is where the common law's
    freedom-of-contract premises meet their statutory limit.
  </p>

  <h2>Vitiating factors and discharge</h2>
  <p>
    Genuine consent has defences. <strong>Misrepresentation</strong> (false statement inducing
    the contract) makes it voidable, with the remedy grade depending on the lie: fraudulent
    (tort damages, all losses), negligent (statutory measure), innocent (rescission or
    indemnity). <strong>Duress and undue influence</strong> pollute consent (the equity
    courtroom's classic: the bank and the elderly guarantor — presumed undue influence shifts
    the burden once the relationship and transaction raise the flag). <strong>Mistake</strong>
    (rarely successful: shared mistake about a fundamental fact — the ship that had already
    sailed in <em>Couturier v Hastie</em>) and <strong>frustration</strong> (performance becomes
    impossible or radically different — venue burns down, war closes the trade, the
    <em>Suez</em> canal cases) discharge obligations without breach, with the Law Reform
    (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 unbundling money paid and work done. Intention, capacity
    (minors bound only for necessaries and beneficial contracts) and legality (contracts to
    commit crime are void, and courts will not enforce bargains tainted by illegality) complete
    the formation-adjacent rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Remedies: the economics of breach</h2>
  <p>
    The default remedy is <strong>expectation damages</strong>: money placing the innocent party
    where performance would have — the market value of the promised benefit, minus savings, plus
    consequential losses that pass <strong>remoteness</strong> (<em>Hadley v Baxendale</em>'s two
    limbs: losses arising naturally, or within the parties' contemplation given known special
    circumstances — the famous mill-shaft case where hidden special knowledge was held <em>not</em>
    notified) and <strong>mitigation</strong> (you must take reasonable steps to reduce loss; you
    recover what you reasonably could not avoid). <strong>Worked example:</strong> a builder
    contracts to refurbish a property worth £300,000 upon completion, for £200,000. He walks off
    80% done. Cost to complete: £60,000. Damages = cost of curing the breach = £60,000 (the
    buyer ends up with the promised £300,000 property having paid £260,000 total) — not the
    £100,000 contract price, and not nothing. Where the loss is market-value based, the formula
    is market price minus contract price. <strong>Specific performance</strong> (equity's order
    to perform) is discretionary and reserved for cases where money is inadequate — land, rare
    goods, never ordinary services (courts will not supervise employment by injunction).
    <strong>Liquidated damages clauses</strong> are enforced if a genuine pre-estimate of loss,
    struck down as penalties if out of all proportion (<em>Dunlop v New Garage</em>'s classic
    test, recalibrated by <em>Cavendish v Makdessi</em> to protect legitimate business
    interests). Reliance damages, restitution and the creditors' other remedies round out the
    toolkit.
  </p>

  <h2>Consumer contracts: the statute's override</h2>
  <p>
    Modern consumer law rewires the formation-and-terms analysis where bargaining power
    diverges. The Consumer Rights Act 2015 (England) implies terms into goods, digital-content
    and services contracts — satisfactory quality, fitness for purpose, conformity with
    description — with tiered remedies (the short-term right to reject within 30 days, then
    repair or replacement, then price reduction) that no exclusion clause can remove. Unfair
    terms legislation (the 2015 Act's Part 2; the EU's Unfair Contract Terms Directive
    heritage) voids terms that were not individually negotiated and cause significant
    imbalance in bad faith — transparency duties, plain-language requirements, and the
    grey-list of presumptively unfair terms (excessive cancellation fees, unilateral variation
    clauses). The doctrinal story: the common law's freedom of contract presumed parties of
    equal strength; the statute corrects the presumption where it fails — the same
    philosophical move consumer-credit regulation (disclosure plus affordability checks) and
    digital-services regimes make. The practical reading for the advanced student: in consumer
    disputes, start from the statutory floor and treat the written terms as the deviation to
    be justified.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Efficient breach and its discontents.</strong> Law-and-economics (Posner) observes:
    if performing costs a promisor 100 and the promisee values performance at 60, breach and
    pay 60 maximises wealth — so expectation damages are the efficient rule, deliberately
    calibrated. The doctrinal pushback is serious: English law's structure (penalty doctrine,
    bad-faith breach, specific performance in kind) protects the promise as such, and the
    <em>Cavendish</em> recalibration shows courts willing to enforce sums protecting legitimate
    interests beyond compensation — the debate is where law's promise-keeping function meets
    economics' efficiency criterion, and it is not settled. <strong>Relational contract
    theory</strong> (Macneil): long-term commercial relationships (franchises, joint ventures,
    supply chains) are governed as much by norms (reciprocity, flexibility, good faith) as by
    discrete terms; English law, long resistant, has let <em>good faith</em> obligations into
    relational contracts (<em>Yam Seng</em>, refined in <em>Bates v Post Office</em> — the
    Post Office litigation's contract-law lessons are a modern masterclass in implied terms,
    relational duties and the limits of paper).
    <strong>Standardisation and the death of consent.</strong> Modern contracts are mass-produced
    terms of service nobody reads — the doctrinal responses (unfair-terms regimes, Plain-language
    requirements, the EU's Digital Services framework) are attempts to keep the consent
    fiction usable; the behavioural research (contract comprehension near zero) is the standing
    challenge to the entire model. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how smart contracts
    (self-executing code) fit offer-acceptance doctrine (code performs without courts — but who
    is the promisor, and what is the remedy when the code is wrong?); whether AI-drafted and
    AI-negotiated terms need new defaults; and how English common law and civil-code systems
    (with their doctrinally different consideration, causa and good-faith structures) converge
    in international commercial practice — the CISG and arbitration are quietly building a
    global contract law that no single jurisdiction controls.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interpretation deserves its own paragraph because it decides more disputes than formation
    does. The modern English approach balances textual and contextual reading: the words
    chosen matter, but so does commercial common sense — the court reads the contract as a
    whole, against its background, to answer what a reasonable person with the parties'
    knowledge would understand the language to mean, while refusing to rewrite bargains
    because hindsight shows them unwise. The implied-terms machinery (terms implied in law
    into classes of contract; implied in fact through business efficacy or the officious
    bystander) fills gaps; contra proferentem reads ambiguity against drafters; and the
    entire edifice assumes parties choosing their own risk allocations — which is why the
    standardisation and consumer overrides above are not add-ons but a structural tension
    running through the modern law: interpretation doctrine presumes bespoke bargains, and
    most contracts are no longer bespoke.
  </p>

